[req] tool_call  call_4 subagent({"agent":"librarian","description":"Verify NZ policy anchors","model":"openrouter/anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5","background":true,"prompt":"NOTE: A previous attempt at this task aborted mid-run due to a provider usage limit; assume NO prior results and start fresh.\n\nCONTEXT: I am writing an executive briefing paper for New Zealand government political stakeholders (Ministers, MPs, political advisors, and agency Chief Procurement Officers at MBIE, Treasury, DIA) about a sovereign AI platform that helps NZ SMEs bid for government tenders via GETS. Every policy citation must be accurate because ministerial advisers will read it. Where the status changed under the current (2023–2026 coalition) government, say so.\n\nTASK: Verify each item below. For each, return: (a) the verified fact in 1–3 sentences, (b) a source URL, and (c) a \"misconception flag\" if the commonly-stated version is wrong or imprecise. Explicitly flag anything you could not verify.\n\n1. NZ Government Procurement Rules — Rule 8 (\"Economic Benefits to New Zealand\" / delivering broader outcomes): what it actually requires of agencies, and the current version/status of the Rules (any 2024–2026 amendments or reviews). VERIFY THIS SPECIFIC CLAIM from our internal regulatory crosswalk: \"the 5th-edition Rules are effective 1 December 2025 and require a minimum 10% weighting for Economic Benefits to New Zealand for goods/services contracts above $100,000 (construction above $9m)\". Confirm or correct the edition number, effective date, thresholds, and the 10% figure.\n2. The former \"15%\" domestic SME procurement target: exact original wording and origin (Broader Outcomes priority \"Supporting domestic SMEs and the construction sector\"?), and — CRITICAL — its CURRENT status as of late 2026 under the coalition government's 5th-edition Rules: retired? replaced by Rule 8's 10% economic-benefit weighting? still reported anywhere? Our internal crosswalk asserts the 15% quota \"no longer carries\" as policy; confirm or correct.\n3. \"Tell us once\" / \"Tell the Government Once\": the canonical programme name and custodian (Better for Business? MBIE? DIA?), the New Zealand Business Number (NZBN)'s role in it, and evidence of cross-party support.\n4. Algorithm Charter for Aotearoa New Zealand (July 2020): the actual commitments, listed accurately (how many are there — six or more?).\n5. NZISM (New Zealand Information Security Manual, current version ~3.8) chapter mapping: what does Chapter 14 actually cover? And which chapters/sections cover certification & accreditation, identification/authentication/access control, cryptography, and event logging (§16.6)? Do NOT assume — verify (e.g. against nzism.govt.nz). I need correct citations for a briefing about an IN CONFIDENCE-class system.\n6. Total annual NZ government procurement spend: the current credible figure + source (is ~$51B/yr still right? what does MBIE officially state?).\n7. ISO/IEC 42001:2023 — a precise one-line definition.\n8. Te Mana Raraunga — one line: what it is, and what the Māori Data Sovereignty Principles Charter says.\n9. Amotai — one line: what it is (NZ supplier diversity certification for Māori & Pasifika businesses), who operates it.\n10. NZ Protective Security Requirements (PSR) / Government Communications Security Bureau classifications: where IN CONFIDENCE and SENSITIVE sit in the classification hierarchy (vs RESTRICTED / SECRET / TOP SECRET), and when commercial-in-confidence procurement data is typically marked.\n11. GETS (Government Electronic Tenders Service): who operates it, approximate scale (live notices/tenders per year), and any published evidence of SME burden (registration complexity, response effort, low SME participation rates).\n12. GCDO (Government Chief Digital Officer): which agency it sits in, and its remit over public-service AI/algorithm assurance.\n\nOUTPUT: a numbered list matching the 12 items; each = verified fact (1–3 sentences) + source URL. End with a \"COULD NOT VERIFY\" list. Accuracy over speed — this feeds a document read by political advisors."})
