[req] tool_call  call_50 subagent({"agent":"technical-writer","background":true,"description":"Draft political briefing paper","model":"dspark/qwen3.8-flash-next","prompt":"You are drafting a publication-ready **Executive Strategic Proposal & Briefing Paper** for TenderPulse NZ, intended for a trusted conduit to share with New Zealand political stakeholders (Ministers, MPs, political advisors) and agency Chief Procurement Officers (MBIE, Treasury, DIA). Voice: a senior NZ Government Relations Director / Public Policy Strategist / GovTech executive — executive, authoritative, commercially astute, NON-PARTISAN. Frame the software as an enabling public-interest utility, not startup hype.\n\nWRITE THE FILE TO: /Users/sean/Desktop/TenderPulseNZ/docs/briefings/EXECUTIVE_POLITICAL_PROPOSAL.md (create the directory if needed). Clean Markdown only. Target ~3,000–3,500 words of body content. ZERO placeholders, TODOs, lorem ipsum, or square-bracket tokens — publication-ready on first pass. Use NZ/British spelling (\"organisation\", \"programme\").\n\n## 1. Required document shell (repo house style, then follow the user's five-section structure exactly)\n- Title line: `# Executive Government & Political Briefing Proposal — TenderPulse NZ`\n- Then a `## Document Control` markdown table with: Document ID `TPNZ-BRIEF-001`; Title; Version `1.0`; Status `Final — for ministerial and Chief Procurement Officer distribution`; Product `TenderPulse NZ`; Steward `Government Relations, TenderPulse NZ`; Date `5 October 2026`; Related Documents `TPNZ-FRD-001 (Functional Requirements Document) · TPNZ-SSP-001 (System Security Plan) · TPNZ-AIMS-00 (NZ Government Regulatory Crosswalk) · ISO/IEC 42001:2023 AIMS suite`.\n- Immediately after the table, a blockquote protective-marking line, e.g.: `> **Protective marking: IN CONFIDENCE.** This paper references commercially sensitive supplier and procurement data handled under the NZ Government Security Classification System; it is suitable for distribution to Ministers, advisors, and agency Chief Procurement Officers.`\n- Then the five sections below.\n\n## 2. Required structure & content\n\n### Section 1 — Executive Summary & Policy Opportunity\n- Government procures approximately **$51.5 billion a year — around 20 per cent of GDP** (figure published by MBIE / New Zealand Government Procurement). Attribute it that way; do not present it as a precise current-year actual, and do not claim it is the value of GETS-advertised contracts (GETS covers only above-threshold open procurements and also carries local-government notices).\n- The challenge: small Kiwi contractors — the backbone of the economy — are structurally locked out. Evidence, attributed: MBIE's 2019 Small Business Strategy found small businesses \"often find themselves shut out of government procurement processes by unnecessarily complex and lengthy processes even for relatively low value contracts\"; MBIE/NZGP's 2021 Procurement Business Survey found the main factors reducing bid effectiveness were complicated procurement processes (25%), lack of agency engagement (20%) and complex information (15%), and only 15% of businesses said they \"always\" have sufficient time to respond. 80-page RFx packs, RealMe-verified GETS registration friction, and no dedicated bid-writing teams. Do NOT invent an annual GETS notice count (MBIE publishes none; \"tens of thousands of notices since 2014\" may be used only if needed at all).\n- The solution: TenderPulse NZ as an automated, sovereign bridge — deconstructing complex tenders into accessible lots, holding verified compliance evidence once, and generating grounded, cited, compliant draft responses. Design target, stated as such: an SME with 5–20 staff and no dedicated bid manager moves from a GETS alert to a first-pass compliant draft in under one working day.\n- Policy payoff: faster procurement velocity, higher SME participation, regional economic stimulus, and immediately better Rule 8 economic-benefit reporting — without lowering procurement quality standards.\n\n### Section 2 — Strategic Alignment with National Policy Mandates\nInclude ONE policy-to-feature mapping table (Government mandate → current policy instrument → TenderPulse capability → measurable outcome). Sub-themes:\n- **SME economic growth & Rule 8.** The 5th-edition Government Procurement Rules took effect **1 December 2025** (rule count cut from 71 to 47; the 4th edition's Broader Outcomes rules — construction skills, quality employment outcomes (18A), worker conditions, net-zero/waste — were consolidated into Rule 8 \"Economic benefit to New Zealand\"). At or above **$100,000** (goods/services/refurbishment) and **$9 million** (new construction works), agencies must seek economic benefits, apply a **minimum 10 per cent weighting for economic benefits in evaluation**, include contract provisions, and monitor delivery; below threshold, the expectation is to award to capable NZ businesses including SMEs. (Note: the dollar thresholds are not new — they date from the 4th edition, effective 2019.) TenderPulse unbundles monolithic tenders into lots, matches regional suppliers by capability, and turns vague economic-benefit claims into cited, auditable evidence agencies can monitor.\n- **CRITICAL honesty on the 15% SME-share ambition.** It is NOT current government policy and must never be stated as such. The 15% figure is a Labour election commitment (2017-era wording, re-published 9 August 2026 under \"Small Business First\", which also brands a \"Tell the government once\" commitment). The former quantified Māori-business targets under Te Kupenga Hao Māori (Progressive Procurement — formerly 5% then 8%) are of uncertain current status under the coalition. Present it non-partisanly and durably: both major parties now compete on SME access — National's instrument is Rule 8's mandatory 10% economic-benefit weighting plus NZBN simplification; Labour's is a 15% SME-share target. Position TenderPulse as the measurement-and-enablement machinery whichever target an administration adopts: it makes SME participation and Rule 8 delivery reportable, auditable data rather than anecdote.\n- **Administrative burden reduction — \"Tell the Government Once\".** The canonical NZ mechanism is the **New Zealand Business Number (NZBN)**, a 13-digit identifier with verified Primary Business Data administered by **MBIE** as Registrar under the **NZBN Act 2012**, created on the \"once only\" / \"tell government once\" principle; launched in 2014 under National's Better Public Services Result 9 \"Better for Business\". Frame cross-party durability (2012 Act and 2014 launch under National; continued under Labour; Labour's 2026 policy branding), but no formal all-party accord. MBIE's own 2024 regulatory impact statement concedes uptake \"has remained patchy\" — TenderPulse operationalises the promise: enter the NZBN once and the platform auto-fills legal and trading names, entity type, registration and GST status/number, registered addresses, and directorship details (13-digit GS1 check-digit validated before any call). House phrase: \"never re-type government-held data\". Director declarations sit in the human sign-off workflow, not as auto-filled credentials.\n- **Data sovereignty & cybersecurity.** Contrast uncontrolled public AI tools — where proprietary commercial pricing, margins and state procurement data can leave New Zealand into foreign cloud large language models with unknown retention — against TenderPulse's sovereign on-premise deployment: all inference performed onshore on dedicated local hardware, **inline hardware DRAM encryption (AES-256-XTS across the unified memory, resistant to cold-boot and physical bus sniffing)**, inference started with **zero request logging** (vLLM flags `--no-enable-log-requests` and `--no-log-stats`, container log driver disabled), mutual-TLS-only reachability of the inference node, single-origin delivery, HSTS preload with a restrictive CSP, and AES-256 encrypted, GPG-signed backups. For compliance framing cite the **current NZISM v3.9** (published April 2025; live header November 2025), not 3.8, and use the CORRECT chapters: **Chapter 4 — System Certification and Accreditation** (agency heads accredit systems up to RESTRICTED), **Chapter 16 — Authentication and Access Controls** (incl. §16.7 multi-factor authentication), **Chapter 17 — Cryptography** (§17.2 approved algorithms, §17.3 approved protocols), and **§16.6 — Event Monitoring, Logging and Auditing**. Explicitly avoid \"NZISM Chapter 14\" (that chapter is Software security). Keep agency roles correct: **GCSB owns the NZISM; NZSIS (Government Protective Security Lead) owns the Protective Security Requirements; Stats NZ (Government Chief Data Steward) convenes the Algorithm Charter; MBIE owns procurement policy.** On classification: the NZ Government Security Classification System has two tiers — policy/privacy (IN-CONFIDENCE, then SENSITIVE) and national security (RESTRICTED, CONFIDENTIAL, SECRET, TOP SECRET). Commercial-in-confidence procurement data is handled at **IN-CONFIDENCE** as the standard baseline (consistent with the Rules' supplier-information protections), escalating to SENSITIVE only where compromise would damage wider interests; note TenderPulse marks bid data IN-CONFIDENCE/SENSITIVE at intake, and that its System Security Plan control crosswalk is maintained against the NZISM baseline and being refreshed to the v3.9 release.\n- **Trust & ethical AI — the Algorithm Charter for Aotearoa New Zealand (July 2020).** It is a **voluntary** charter convened by **Stats NZ / the Government Chief Data Steward** containing **six commitments: Transparency; Partnership; People; Data; Privacy, Ethics and Human Rights; and Human Oversight** (21 agencies signed at launch). Map TenderPulse controls to each commitment (e.g. cited sentences → transparency; grounded RAG + Amotai verification → data integrity; mandatory human sign-off gates → human oversight). Also note honestly that assurance authority is split: the Algorithm Charter (Stats NZ/GCDS), public-service AI adoption and assurance (the **Office of the Government Chief Digital Officer within DIA**, asked by the Public Service Commissioner in July 2023 to lead public-service AI, with Responsible AI GenAI guidance of February 2025 and an AI Assurance Regime in development), and the national AI strategy (MBIE, with a Minister for AI). Do not write that the GCDO owns the Charter.\n- **Te Tiriti and Māori data sovereignty.** **Te Mana Raraunga is the Māori Data Sovereignty Network** (formed 2015; Charter approved April 2016; not a government agency): Māori data is a living tāonga subject to te Tiriti and UNDRIP rights and Māori governance, guided by rangatiratanga (including storing data in Aotearoa), whakapapa (provenance), kotahitanga (collective benefit), whanaungatanga–manaakitanga (including free, prior and informed consent), and kaitiakitanga (guardianship, including tapu/noa access). Note that the public service's operational instrument is the **Te Kāhui Raraunga Māori Data Governance Model (2023)** — keep the two distinct. TenderPulse records iwi/hapū affiliations, Amotai certificates and Pasifika status as consented, revocable vault attributes with tapu/noa access tiers, and keeps all data in-country with provenance lineage. **Amotai** is supplier-diversity **verification** (businesses at least 50% Māori- and/or Pasifika-owned, NZBN-registered) established by **Auckland Council in 2018** and operating nationally (approximately 2,700 verified suppliers, ~175 buyer members), formerly contracted by Te Puni Kōkiri as an intermediary under Progressive Procurement. Call it \"Amotai verification\", never a government certificate; affirmative inclusion means verified Māori and Pasifika suppliers are surfaced and matched, never penalised by the scoring.\n\n### Section 3 — Solution Overview & Technical Assurance (In Plain Government Terms)\nFour subsections, readable by a non-technical Minister:\n- **3.1 Tender decomposition & opportunity matching:** the platform monitors GETS (gets.govt.nz, managed by MBIE/NZGP) every 15 minutes, retrieves and parses PDF/DOCX/XLSX attachments (OCR confidence flagged for scanned pages), and uses AI-assisted decomposition to break monolithic RFPs into lots, mandatory gates (certifications, insurances, H&S, te Tiriti) and evaluation criteria — including Rule 8 social-procurement flags — validated against a typed contract. Opportunity matching scores each supplier–lot pair on semantic fit (70%) and criteria coverage (30%), capped at 70% where a mandatory gate fails, with supplier alerts at ≥80% match.\n- **3.2 The \"Single Vault\" digital credential store:** verified insurances (cover type, insurer, policy number, cover amount, expiry), H&S policies, financial statements, policies, case studies, and te Tiriti commitments are uploaded once, held in a private tenant-scoped evidence vault, and reused across every bid; expiry alerts at 30/14/7 days and 24 hours. This is the working implementation of \"tell the Government once\" at business level.\n- **3.3 Grounded bid copilot & human-in-the-loop verification:** every factual capability claim in a draft must cite a specific vault evidence chunk; an implemented hallucination barrier discards any citation that does not match actually-retrieved evidence, and uncited statements are surfaced as \"unverified\". Named human sign-off is mandatory on the sections where errors cost real money — pricing, te Tiriti commitments, Rule 8 social-procurement, and the H&S statement — and commercial pricing/margin approval is a hard prerequisite before export unlocks; approver identity and timestamp are persisted. Exports (DOCX and PDF) carry citations as footnotes and an evidence-traceability appendix. Be honest: faithfulness release thresholds (RAG Triad ≥0.90 faithfulness / ≥0.98 relevance / ≥0.90 correctness) are the platform's ISO 42001 AI-management-system release policy; the cite-to-source barrier, unverified flagging, and sign-off gates are implemented. Do NOT present tamper-evident hash-chained audit logging or cryptographically signed citations as live features — they are roadmap/design items (FRD FR-601).\n- **3.4 Security & architectural safeguards (plain English):** per-tenant row-level security keyed to the authenticated tenant context (no cross-supplier visibility); private evidence storage path-scoped per organisation; a two-host on-premise topology — application and data plane on one host, AI inference on a separate onshore AI node reachable only over mutual TLS with pinned client certificates, unreachable from outside the network; the inference image pinned by cryptographic digest; hardware memory encryption and zero request logging as above; server-side-only reach to inference (never exposed to a browser); encrypted nightly backups; a verification suite that audits the AI node's TPM attestation, memory-encryption posture, logging flags and mTLS enforcement. ISO/IEC 42001:2023 — the first international certifiable **AI management system** standard (a governance/management-system standard, like ISO 27001 for AI governance, not a security standard) — governs the AI lifecycle, with a documented risk register (factual fabrication rated Critical; cross-border disclosure tracked), impact assessment, monitoring and incident response.\n\n### Section 4 — Public Value, Economic Impact & Pilot Proof\n- Economic impact: retaining public spend within regional New Zealand; lowering the cost of bidding for trades, construction and tech firms; converting Rule 8 obligations from narrative into monitored outcomes.\n- Government efficiency: standardised, compliant responses landing on procurement desks — fewer disqualified or non-responsive submissions, faster evaluation, and better SME-pipeline reporting for Rule 8 monitoring and for any future SME-share measurement (use neutral KPIs like \"SME participation rate\", never \"15% attainment\").\n- Pilot proof honesty: describe implemented capability (end-to-end ingest pipeline validation, decomposition, vault ingestion, citation barrier, sign-off gates) and design targets (SME with 5–20 staff; under one working day) as proof of readiness; do NOT claim existing agency pilots, supplier win-rate uplift statistics, or client names.\n- Pilot roadmap: a low-risk **90-day sandbox pilot** with **1–2 regional councils or select central ministries** and **10–20 local contractors** (including Amotai-verified suppliers), success measures around SME onboarding friction, bid completeness and compliance, Rule 8 reportability, and evaluation-officer time saved. No pilot programme currently exists in TenderPulse documentation — present it as proposed, not underway.\n\n### Section 5 — Asks & Proposed Engagement Pathways\nThree clear, actionable, non-presumptuous next steps, numbered: (1) facilitate a strategic briefing with MBIE Procurement Policy (New Zealand Government Procurement), the GCDO (DIA), and the Ministry for Small Business team; (2) explore a sponsored regional or ministerial pilot to test SME onboarding under current GETS workflows; (3) formal presentation of the TenderPulse NZ System Security Plan (TPNZ-SSP-001) and the ISO 42001 / NZISM compliance crosswalk (aligned to v3.9) to government technical assessors. Close with a short blockquote \"Suggested next step\" (a 45-minute policy briefing at the recipient's convenience) and a dignified closing line.\n\n## 3. Style rules\n- Executive, plain-government prose; short paragraphs; numbered lists where sequencing helps; callout blockquotes used sparingly (protective marking, key policy payoff, suggested next step). Maximum TWO tables in the whole document.\n- No invented statistics beyond the verified figures given above; no \"X notices per year\"; no client or agency names not given here; no hype adjectives (\"revolutionary\", \"game-changing\").\n- Every figure or policy statement above must appear exactly as attributed (source name + figure). If a nuance conflicts with your instincts, the verified anchor wins.\n\n## 4. Explicit \"DO NOT CLAIM\" list (from repo gap analysis)\n- 15% target as current policy; all four sign-off gates G-1…G-4 as live (pricing gate + four mandatory review sections are implemented; G-2/3/4 separation-of-duties is deferred design); hash-chained/tamper-evident audit log or \"cryptographically signed citations\" as live; Amotai \"+5 scoring boost\" or bias-parity monitor as live (current scorer applies no additive boosts — phrase affirmative inclusion as surfacing/matching, never penalising); RAG Triad thresholds as enforced in production (policy thresholds); email/Slack alerts as live (design); multi-factor scoring v2 as live (deferred); automated nightly NZBN refresh/credential expiry automation as live (on-demand NZBN resolution is live; expiry tracking is manual metadata); any existing pilot programme.\n\nDeliverable: write the file, then reply with the file path, a 3-line summary, and any judgement calls you made."})
