[req] tool_call  call_55 write({"path":"/Users/sean/Desktop/TenderPulseNZ/docs/briefings/EXECUTIVE_POLITICAL_PROPOSAL.md","content":"# Executive Government & Political Briefing Proposal — TenderPulse NZ\n\n## Document Control\n\n| Attribute | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Document ID | TPNZ-BRIEF-001 |\n| Title | Executive Government & Political Briefing Proposal — TenderPulse NZ |\n| Version | 1.0 |\n| Status | Final — for ministerial and Chief Procurement Officer distribution |\n| Product | TenderPulse NZ |\n| Steward | Government Relations, TenderPulse NZ |\n| Date | 5 October 2026 |\n| Related Documents | TPNZ-FRD-001 (Functional Requirements Document) · TPNZ-SSP-001 (System Security Plan) · TPNZ-AIMS-00 (NZ Government Regulatory Crosswalk) · ISO/IEC 42001:2023 AIMS suite |\n\n> **Protective marking: IN CONFIDENCE.** This paper references commercially sensitive supplier and procurement data handled under the NZ Government Security Classification System; it is suitable for distribution to Ministers, advisors, and agency Chief Procurement Officers.\n\n## Section 1 — Executive Summary & Policy Opportunity\n\nGovernment is one of New Zealand's largest single buyers. The figure published by MBIE / New Zealand Government Procurement is that the public sector procures approximately **$51.5 billion a year — around 20 per cent of GDP**. That is a published order-of-magnitude estimate, not a precise current-year actual, and it is larger than the value of contracts advertised on the Government Electronic Tender Service (GETS): GETS covers only above-threshold open procurements and also carries local-government notices. Even on the most conservative reading, roughly a fifth of the national economy is allocated through processes designed around large agencies and large suppliers.\n\nThe problem is not demand. It is access. Small Kiwi contractors — the trades, small construction firms and regional technology companies that are the backbone of the economy — are structurally locked out of the processes that distribute this spend. MBIE's 2019 Small Business Strategy found that small businesses \"often find themselves shut out of government procurement processes by unnecessarily complex and lengthy processes even for relatively low value contracts\". MBIE and New Zealand Government Procurement's 2021 Procurement Business Survey corroborated the lived experience: the factors most frequently reducing bid effectiveness were complicated procurement processes (25 per cent), lack of agency engagement (20 per cent) and complex information (15 per cent), and only 15 per cent of businesses said they \"always\" have sufficient time to respond. The mechanics are familiar to anyone who has tried to bid: 80-page RFx packs, RealMe-verified GETS registration friction before a supplier can even express interest, and no dedicated bid-writing team at the small end of the economy. Large incumbent suppliers employ people to read tenders for a living. The twelve-staff plumbing firm does not.\n\nTenderPulse NZ is an automated, sovereign bridge across that gap. It deconstructs complex tenders into accessible lots and plain-language gates, holds a supplier's verified compliance evidence once in a private vault, and generates grounded, cited, compliant draft responses that a small business owner can read, correct and sign — not a black-box answer, but an honest first draft in which every factual claim is traceable to evidence. Stated as a design target, not an observed result: an SME with five to twenty staff and no dedicated bid manager moves from a GETS alert to a first-pass compliant draft in under one working day.\n\n> **Policy payoff:** faster procurement velocity, higher SME participation, regional economic stimulus, and immediately better Rule 8 economic-benefit reporting — achieved without lowering procurement quality standards, because the platform enforces compliance rather than excusing it.\n\nNothing here asks government to buy differently, or to lower a bar. It asks what small businesses have argued for a decade: that the machinery of procurement be made navigable at the cost of a software licence, on New Zealand hardware, under New Zealand law.\n\n## Section 2 — Strategic Alignment with National Policy Mandates\n\nTenderPulse NZ does not propose a new policy. It supplies instrumentation for mandates already on the books — and for the SME-access competition both major parties are now running on. The mapping below anchors each mandate to its current policy instrument, the corresponding platform capability, and the outcome an agency can measure.\n\n| Government mandate | Current policy instrument | TenderPulse capability | Measurable outcome |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| SME economic growth and broader outcomes | Government Procurement Rules (5th edition, effective 1 December 2025): Rule 8 \"Economic benefit to New Zealand\", minimum 10 per cent economic-benefit weighting | Tender decomposition into lots; regional supplier matching by capability; cited economic-benefit claims | Rule 8 commitments evaluated at weighting and monitored in delivery as auditable evidence, not narrative |\n| SME share of government procurement (instrument varies by administration) | Rule 8's 10 per cent weighting and NZBN simplification; a 15 per cent SME-share commitment under Labour's \"Small Business First\" (August 2026) | SME-participation data drawn from vault and bid records | Any SME-share target an administration adopts becomes reportable and auditable rather than anecdotal |\n| Administrative burden reduction — \"Tell the Government Once\" | NZBN Act 2012; MBIE as Registrar; \"once only\" principle | One NZBN entry auto-fills verified Primary Business Data | Suppliers never re-type government-held data; declarations remain human sign-offs |\n| Data sovereignty and cybersecurity | NZISM v3.9 (Chapters 4, 16 and 17; §16.6); NZ Government Security Classification System | Onshore inference with hardware memory encryption, zero request logging, mutual-TLS-only reachability | Commercial-in-confidence procurement data stays in New Zealand and never enters a foreign cloud model |\n| Trust and ethical AI in the public service | Algorithm Charter for Aotearoa New Zealand (convened by Stats NZ/GCDS, July 2020); Responsible AI guidance (GCDO, DIA); ISO/IEC 42001:2023 AIMS | Cited sentences, grounded retrieval with a citation barrier, mandatory human sign-off gates | Each Charter commitment maps to a named, verifiable platform control |\n| Te Tiriti obligations and Māori data sovereignty | Te Mana Raraunga principles; Te Kāhui Raraunga Māori Data Governance Model (2023); Amotai verification | Consented, revocable attributes with tapu/noa access tiers; in-country storage with provenance lineage | Verified Māori and Pasifika suppliers surfaced and matched, never penalised by scoring |\n\n### Economic benefit to New Zealand: the Rule 8 settlement\n\nThe 5th edition of the Government Procurement Rules took effect on **1 December 2025**. The rule count was cut from 71 to 47, and the 4th edition's Broader Outcomes rules — construction skills, quality employment outcomes (Rule 18A), worker conditions, and net-zero and waste commitments — were consolidated into a single Rule 8, \"Economic benefit to New Zealand\". At or above **$100,000** for goods, services and refurbishment, and **$9 million** for new construction works, agencies must seek economic benefits, apply a **minimum 10 per cent weighting for economic benefits in evaluation**, include contract provisions, and monitor delivery. Below those thresholds, the expectation is to award to capable New Zealand businesses, including SMEs. The dollar thresholds themselves are not new — they date from the 4th edition, effective 2019. What has changed is the concentration of the obligation, and with it the evidence burden: claims must now carry enough weight to survive a 10 per cent evaluation criterion and enough specificity to be monitored in delivery. TenderPulse unbundles monolithic tenders into lots, matches regional suppliers by capability, and turns vague economic-benefit claims into cited, auditable evidence agencies can actually monitor.\n\n### SME-share ambitions: stated precisely, so the paper ages well across administrations\n\nOne figure requires care, because it is frequently misreported. The ambition that **15 per cent** of government procurement should flow to SMEs is **not current government policy** and must never be cited as such. It is a Labour election commitment — 2017-era wording, republished on 9 August 2026 under the party's \"Small Business First\" policy, which also brands a \"Tell the government once\" commitment. Separately, the former quantified Māori-business targets under Te Kupenga Hao Māori (Progressive Procurement — formerly 5 per cent, later 8 per cent) are of uncertain current status under the coalition.\n\nThe durable, non-partisan reading is that both major parties now compete on SME access, with different instruments: National's is Rule 8's mandatory 10 per cent economic-benefit weighting plus NZBN simplification; Labour's is the 15 per cent SME-share target. TenderPulse is deliberately positioned as the **measurement-and-enablement machinery for whichever target an administration adopts**: it makes SME participation and Rule 8 delivery reportable, auditable data rather than anecdote. A change of government changes the headline number; it does not change the need for the instrumentation.\n\n### \"Tell the Government Once\": from principle to working software\n\nThe canonical New Zealand mechanism for cutting administrative burden is the **New Zealand Business Number (NZBN)**: a 13-digit identifier with verified Primary Business Data, administered by **MBIE as Registrar under the NZBN Act 2012**, created on the \"once only\" — tell government once — principle. The NZBN launched in 2014 under National's Better Public Services Result 9, \"Better for Business\", and continued under the subsequent Labour-led administration; Labour's 2026 policy now brands a \"Tell the government once\" commitment. That cross-party persistence is genuine, though it stops short of a formal all-party accord, and we claim no more than that. What is documented is the gap between promise and uptake: MBIE's own 2024 regulatory impact statement concedes that NZBN uptake \"has remained patchy\".\n\nTenderPulse operationalises the promise precisely where uptake stalled. A supplier enters its NZBN once — the platform validates the 13-digit GS1 check digit before any call is made — and the system auto-fills legal and trading names, entity type, registration and GST status and number, registered addresses, and directorship details from verified Primary Business Data. The working rule inside the platform is simple: **never re-type government-held data**. Director declarations deliberately sit in the human sign-off workflow rather than being auto-filled credentials: a pre-filled form is not a signed declaration, and the platform is built to know the difference.\n\n### Data sovereignty and cybersecurity: keeping State and commercial confidence in New Zealand\n\nThe contrast with uncontrolled public AI tools is the clearest argument this platform has. When a small firm pastes its cost build-up into a public chatbot, proprietary commercial pricing, margins and State procurement data leave New Zealand — into foreign cloud large language models with unknown retention and unknown reuse. TenderPulse is the opposite architecture by design: a sovereign, on-premise deployment in which **all inference is performed onshore on dedicated local hardware**. Three protections carry the argument. First, **inline hardware DRAM encryption — AES-256-XTS across the unified memory** — makes prompts and outputs unreadable to cold-boot attacks and physical bus sniffing. Second, the inference service starts with **zero request logging**: it runs with vLLM's `--no-enable-log-requests` and `--no-log-stats` flags and the container log driver disabled, so a tender and its draft exist only in encrypted memory. Third, the inference node is reachable **only over mutual TLS**, from the application server alone — never from a browser, never from outside the network. Delivery is single-origin with HSTS preload and a restrictive CSP; backups are AES-256 encrypted and GPG-signed.\n\nCompliance is framed against the **current NZISM v3.9** (published April 2025; live header November 2025), using the correct chapters: **Chapter 4 — System Certification and Accreditation**, under which agency heads accredit systems up to RESTRICTED; **Chapter 16 — Authentication and Access Controls**, including §16.7 on multi-factor authentication; **Chapter 17 — Cryptography**, with §17.2 on approved algorithms and §17.3 on approved protocols; and **§16.6 — Event Monitoring, Logging and Auditing**. The agency roles are equally precise: the **GCSB owns the NZISM**; **NZSIS**, as Government Protective Security Lead, owns the Protective Security Requirements; **Stats NZ**, as Government Chief Data Steward, convenes the Algorithm Charter; **MBIE owns procurement policy**.\n\nOn classification: the NZ Government Security Classification System has two tiers — policy/privacy (IN-CONFIDENCE, then SENSITIVE) and national security (RESTRICTED, CONFIDENTIAL, SECRET, TOP SECRET). Commercial-in-confidence procurement data is handled at **IN-CONFIDENCE** as the standard baseline, consistent with the Rules' protections for supplier information, escalating to SENSITIVE only where compromise would damage wider interests. TenderPulse marks bid data IN-CONFIDENCE or SENSITIVE at intake, and its System Security Plan control crosswalk is maintained against the NZISM baseline and is being refreshed to the v3.9 release.\n\n### Trust and ethical AI: the Algorithm Charter, and who actually owns what\n\nThe **Algorithm Charter for Aotearoa New Zealand (July 2020)** is a **voluntary** charter convened by **Stats NZ in its Government Chief Data Steward role**, signed at launch by 21 agencies, and containing **six commitments: Transparency; Partnership; People; Data; Privacy, Ethics and Human Rights; and Human Oversight**. TenderPulse maps its controls directly: cited sentences in every draft serve Transparency; grounded retrieval plus Amotai verification serves the Data commitment by tying claims to verified evidence; consented, in-country handling of supplier data serves the Privacy, Ethics and Human Rights commitment; and the mandatory human sign-off gates are the literal embodiment of Human Oversight. Partnership and People are honoured through the te Tiriti alignment described below and by designing for the small-business user, not the incumbent.\n\nAssurance authority in the public service is genuinely split, and honest papers say so. The Algorithm Charter sits with Stats NZ and the Government Chief Data Steward. Public-service AI adoption and assurance sits with the **Office of the Government Chief Digital Officer within DIA**, which the Public Service Commissioner asked in July 2023 to lead public-service AI, and which has since issued Responsible AI guidance for generative AI (February 2025), with an AI Assurance Regime in development. The national AI strategy sits with MBIE, alongside a Minister for AI. The GCDO does not own the Charter, and TenderPulse presents its controls as contributions to each instrument — not as certification by any single authority.\n\n### Te Tiriti and Māori data sovereignty: network, model, and verification — kept distinct\n\n**Te Mana Raraunga is the Māori Data Sovereignty Network** — formed in 2015, its Charter approved in April 2016 — a network, not a government agency. Its framing is that Māori data is a living tāonga, subject to te Tiriti and UNDRIP rights and to Māori governance, and its guidance runs through rangatiratanga (including storing data in Aotearoa), whakapapa (provenance), kotahitanga (collective benefit), whanaungatanga–manaakitanga (including free, prior and informed consent), and kaitiakitanga (guardianship, including tapu/noa access). The public service's separate operational instrument is the **Te Kāhui Raraunga Māori Data Governance Model (2023)**. The two are related but distinct, and this paper keeps them distinct.\n\nIn implementation, TenderPulse records iwi/hapū affiliations, Amotai certificates and Pasifika status as **consented, revocable vault attributes with tapu/noa access tiers**, holds all data in-country, and maintains provenance lineage. **Amotai** is supplier-diversity **verification** — businesses at least 50 per cent Māori- and/or Pasifika-owned, and NZBN-registered — established by **Auckland Council in 2018** and now operating nationally with approximately 2,700 verified suppliers and around 175 buyer members; it was formerly contracted by Te Puni Kōkiri as an intermediary under Progressive Procurement. It is Amotai verification, never a government certificate, and this paper never calls it one. Affirmative inclusion here has a precise meaning: verified Māori and Pasifika suppliers are surfaced and matched to opportunities; they are never penalised by the scoring.\n\n## Section 3 — Solution Overview & Technical Assurance (In Plain Government Terms)\n\n### 3.1 Tender decomposition and opportunity matching\n\nThe platform monitors GETS (gets.govt.nz, managed by MBIE's New Zealand Government Procurement) every 15 minutes. When a tender appears, it retrieves and parses the PDF, DOCX and XLSX attachments — flagging OCR confidence for scanned pages so nobody trusts a bad scan blindly — and applies AI-assisted decomposition: monolithic RFPs are broken into lots, mandatory gates (certifications, insurances, health and safety, te Tiriti commitments) and evaluation criteria, including Rule 8 social-procurement flags. Every extracted field is validated against a typed contract, so the tender's structure is machine-checked before it is shown to a human. Opportunity matching then scores each supplier–lot pair on semantic fit (70 per cent) and criteria coverage (30 per cent), capped at 70 per cent where a mandatory gate fails — so a supplier is never alerted to an award it cannot legally win — with supplier alerts triggered at matches of 80 per cent and above.\n\n### 3.2 The \"Single Vault\": verified credentials, stored once\n\nVerified insurances (cover type, insurer, policy number, cover amount, expiry), health and safety policies, financial statements, business policies, case studies and te Tiriti commitments are uploaded once into a private, tenant-scoped evidence vault and reused across every bid. The vault issues expiry alerts at 30 days, 14 days, 7 days and 24 hours, against expiry dates maintained as supplier-provided metadata. This is \"tell the Government once\" implemented at business level: the supplier's compliance record is assembled once, kept current, and carried into every response.\n\n### 3.3 Grounded bid copilot and human-in-the-loop verification\n\nEvery factual capability claim in a draft must cite a specific vault evidence chunk. An implemented hallucination barrier discards any citation that does not match evidence actually retrieved, and uncited statements are surfaced as \"unverified\" rather than quietly shipped. Named human sign-off is mandatory on the sections where errors cost real money — pricing, te Tiriti commitments, Rule 8 social-procurement, and the health and safety statement — and commercial pricing and margin approval is a hard prerequisite before export unlocks, with approver identity and timestamp persisted. Exported DOCX and PDF carry citations as footnotes plus an evidence-traceability appendix.\n\nTwo honesty notes, because assessors will test them. The RAG Triad release thresholds — faithfulness ≥0.90, relevance ≥0.98, correctness ≥0.90 — are the platform's ISO 42001 AI-management-system release policy, not per-request production enforcement; the cite-to-source barrier, unverified flagging and sign-off gates are what is implemented and running. The pricing approval gate and the four mandatory review sections are live; the formal separation-of-duties gate architecture (design references G-2/3/4) is deferred design, as are tamper-evident hash-chained audit logging and cryptographically signed citations — roadmap items under FRD FR-601, not features in use today.\n\n### 3.4 Security and architectural safeguards, in plain English\n\nPer-tenant row-level security is keyed to the authenticated tenant context: one supplier's evidence is invisible to every other supplier. The evidence vault is a private storage path scoped per organisation. The deployment is a two-host on-premise topology — the application and data plane on one host; AI inference on a separate, onshore AI node reachable only over mutual TLS with pinned client certificates and unreachable from outside the network. The inference image is pinned by cryptographic digest, so the binary audited is the binary run. Hardware memory encryption and zero request logging operate as described above, and inference is reached server-side only, never exposed to a browser. Nightly backups are AES-256 encrypted and GPG-signed. A dedicated verification suite audits the AI node's TPM attestation, memory-encryption posture, logging flags and mutual-TLS enforcement on a schedule.\n\nGovernance sits on **ISO/IEC 42001:2023 — the first international certifiable AI management system standard**: a governance and management-system standard, in the family of ISO/IEC 27001 for AI governance rather than a security standard in itself. It governs the AI lifecycle with a documented risk register — factual fabrication rated Critical, cross-border disclosure tracked — plus impact assessment, monitoring and incident response.\n\n## Section 4 — Public Value, Economic Impact & Pilot Proof\n\nThe economic case is retention, not extraction. Every award won by a regional trades, construction or technology firm keeps public spend circulating inside regional New Zealand instead of defaulting to the few incumbents with bid-writing capacity. The platform lowers the cost of bidding for exactly the sectors the Rules' broader-outcomes agenda targets — trades and construction skills, quality local employment — and converts Rule 8 obligations from supplier narrative into monitored outcomes agencies can evidence in delivery.\n\nGovernment gains on the same transaction. Standardised, compliant responses landing on procurement desks mean fewer disqualified or non-responsive submissions, faster evaluation, and better SME-pipeline reporting — for Rule 8 monitoring now, and for any future SME-share measurement a future government adopts, using neutral indicators such as SME participation rate.\n\nOn proof, this paper tells the truth as it stands. What is implemented and validated is the machinery: end-to-end ingest pipeline validation from GETS retrieval through parsing and decomposition, vault ingestion, the citation barrier, and the sign-off gates. The under-one-working-day figure and the five-to-twenty-staff SME profile are stated design targets — proof of readiness, not observed results. There is no agency pilot running, no supplier win-rate uplift statistic, and no client name cited in this paper, because none exists to cite.\n\nWhat we propose instead is a deliberately low-risk, evidence-first trial:\n\n1. A **90-day sandbox pilot** with **one to two regional councils or select central ministries** and **10–20 local contractors**, including Amotai-verified suppliers.\n2. Success measures fixed before day one: SME onboarding friction; bid completeness and compliance; Rule 8 reportability of submitted claims; and evaluation-officer time saved on non-responsive submissions.\n3. Reporting back to participating agencies with the vault-and-citation data trail, so the pilot produces the measurement instrument as well as its results.\n\nThe pilot programme is proposed, not underway. That is the point: an administration can start it at the pace it chooses, without committing to anything before it sees the evidence.\n\n## Section 5 — Asks & Proposed Engagement Pathways\n\nThree concrete next steps, none of which presumptuous on procurement decisions:\n\n1. **A strategic briefing** convening MBIE Procurement Policy (New Zealand Government Procurement), the Office of the Government Chief Digital Officer (DIA), and the Ministry for Small Business team — one session, one paper, aligned to the mandates in Section 2.\n2. **A sponsored regional or ministerial pilot**, scoped as the 90-day sandbox in Section 4, to test SME onboarding friction under current GETS workflows — before thresholds, before contracts, before commitments beyond observation.\n3. **A formal technical presentation** of the TenderPulse NZ System Security Plan (TPNZ-SSP-001) and the ISO 42001 / NZISM compliance crosswalk (aligned to NZISM v3.9) to government technical assessors, so the security argument is tested by the people who grade it.\n\n> **Suggested next step:** a 45-minute policy briefing at the recipient's convenience, with the System Security Plan and the compliance crosswalk available in advance to officials who wish to read ahead.\n\nTenderPulse NZ asks government only for what the Rules themselves promise: that a capable small New Zealand business can see an opportunity, understand it, and answer it on the evidence — and that the Crown can prove, with data, what that access is worth."})
